course: dictionary
lesson: 11329
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it show you which one the writer means. «The ox wore a yoke on its neck» means a wooden bar, here a thing you can picture. Yoke is a noun in that sentence.
explain: The same word can also be an action. «The farmer yoked the oxen to the plough» means he joined the animals together with a yoke. Ask yourself: is the word a thing, or something someone does? The rest of the sentence gives the clue.
explain: Some meanings are heavy and serious. «They lived under the yoke of a cruel king» means they were controlled and burdened, not that they carried anything. Other meanings are everyday, like the Irish slang in «Pass me that yoke», where a yoke is a gadget whose name you can't remember.
fact: A yoke is a wooden bar fixed across the necks of two animals, usually oxen, so they can pull a plough or cart together.
fact: A yoke can also be a bar across a person's shoulders for carrying two buckets.
fact: To yoke, as a verb, means to join animals with a yoke, or to join two things together.
fact: «The yoke of slavery» means something that controls people or is a heavy burden.
fact: In Irish slang, a «yoke» is any thing or gadget whose name you can't remember.
exercise: «The ox had a yoke on its neck.» What is a yoke in this sentence? | A bell that rings on a farm | *A wooden bar across two animals' necks for pulling | A type of small bird | A kind of hat
exercise: «The farmer yoked the oxen to the plough.» What does yoked mean here? | Lost the animals in a field | Painted the animals in bright colours | Set the animals free from work | *Joined the animals together with a yoke
exercise: Ella in Dublin says, «Pass me that yoke.» What does she mean? | Pass me the bread for dinner | Pass me the sky when it turns grey | *Pass me that gadget, the one whose name I can't remember | Pass me the cow from the field
test: «The people struggled under the yoke of the cruel emperor.» What does yoke mean here? | A warm, woolly jumper | A pair of oxen | *Control and a heavy burden | A bucket for carrying water
test: Which word is a verb in this sentence? «The farmer will yoke the oxen before dawn.» | dawn | *yoke | oxen | farmer
exam: Jesus says, «My yoke is easy and my burden is light.» What does this suggest? | *Following him is not crushing or too heavy | Carrying two heavy buckets is simple | Pulling a plough is the best job | Being lost in a storm is light work
